5630 opencode run --model opencode/minimax-m3.1-flash-free --dir /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue --agent build --title rbops-phase-084 # DO THIS, NOW. No orientation phase.  Your working directory is the project checkout. It is the ONLY place you may read or write. The RBOPS pipeline that invoked you lives elsewhere; do not inspect it, do not audit it, and do not spend a single tool call on it.  You have one job, described below. Work in this order and do not deviate:  1. Reproduce the finding. Smallest command that shows it. Then STOP and note it. 2. Write ONE failing test that demonstrates it. Run ONLY your targeted test: `cargo test <test_name>` (fast). Do NOT run `cargo test --all-targets` during iteration — the full suite runs 67 binaries, takes 15 minutes, and will burn your time budget. Watch your test fail. 3. Make the smallest change that turns that test green. Re-run `cargo test <test_name>`. 4. Add the remaining edge-case tests required below. Test each with `cargo test <test_name>`. 5. Only now run the four gates (full suite at the end):    - cargo fmt --all -- --check    - cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    - cargo test --all-targets    - ../rbops/verify.sh "phase-084" 6. Write REPORT.md.  Budget: reproduce in 2 minutes, first test by 5, first edit by 10. If you still have not written code by then, you have misread the task — re-read this message. Do not browse. Do not read files you have not been told to read. Do not write a plan document. There is no human to review a plan.  Files: read and write ONLY inside your working directory. Never write to /tmp, /home, or anywhere outside the project — the permission system auto-rejects those calls, and in this pipeline a rejected tool call ends your run. If you need scratch space, use ./target/tmp/ inside the project.  When you are done, say DONE and stop.  ---  ## Contract you must obey (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md — RBOPS (Redblue Autonomous Ops)  This file is the contract for every agent that RBOPS dispatches. It is injected verbatim into every phase context by `rbops/dispatch.sh`. It is law, not advice.  You are running inside an automated pipeline. There is no human in your loop. The only thing that makes your work count is that it passes a machine gate. Nothing you *say* about your work is evidence. Only what `rbops/verify.sh` proves is evidence.  ---  ## 0. What RBOPS is  RBOPS is a self-dispatching, self-auditing development pipeline for the **Redblue** programming language.  - **Dispatcher** (`rbops/dispatch.sh`) — picks the next eligible phase from   `rbops/phases.json`, runs it, records state, gates it, triggers the next tick. - **Manifest** (`rbops/phases.json`) — the single source of truth. Machine-readable.   If a phase is not in the manifest, it does not exist. - **Gate** (`rbops/verify.sh`) — the only authority on whether work is real. - **Auditor** (`.opencode/agent/auditor.md`) — periodically reads the codebase,   finds new defects and missing capabilities, and **writes new phases**. - **Bootstrap** — the endgame: Redblue compiles Redblue.  ### The state machine  ```   IDLE ──dispatch──▶ RUNNING ──verify ok──▶ REVIEW ──ok──▶ .done ──▶ next phase                       │                       ├─ verify fail ──▶ RETRY (attempt+1)                       ├─ model error ──▶ .deferred (retry later tick)                       ├─ max attempts ──▶ .blocked (needs human)                       └─ budget spent ──▶ .starved (needs new phases) ```  State is **git**, not a database. Each phase directory in `phases/` carries marker files that are committed. Stateless runners therefore work.  ---  ## 1. HARD RULES — violating any of these fails the phase  1. **Do not touch RBOPS itself.** Never edit `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`,    `rbops/phases.json`, or any `.opencode/agent/*.md`. The bot token has no    `workflows` scope and a push touching `.github/` is refused. Do not waste a    phase discovering this. 2. **Never weaken a gate.** You may not modify `verify.sh`, delete a failing    test, add `#[ignore]`, add `.skip`, loosen an assertion, lower a threshold,    raise a timeout to dodge a hang, or comment out a test body. **Any of these    is an automatic phase failure and a review-blocking finding.** There is no    exception for "it was flaky". Flaky tests get fixed, not muted. 3. **No claiming success you did not achieve.** If `verify.sh` is red, the phase    is not done. Report the failure honestly in `REPORT.md` and exit. 4. **Do not run long builds locally.** CI is the only place `cargo build` runs.    Locally use `cargo check --all-targets` at most, and only if essential. 5. **Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens.** Check `.gitignore` covers    `.env`, `*.key`, `target/`. 6. **No destructive git.** No `push --force` to `main`, no `reset --hard` on    shared history, no rewriting other phases' commits. 7. **One phase, one concern.** If you discover work that does not belong to your    phase, do not do it. Record it in `phases/phase-NNN/FINDINGS.md` so the    auditor can promote it to a real phase. 8. **No unbounded speculation.** Do not rewrite the architecture, rename public    types, change the `.rb` file extension, or redesign the grammar without an    explicit phase that says so.  ---  ## 2. Redblue — what must never silently change  Redblue is a tree-walking interpreter: `Lexer → Parser → Analyzer → VM`. Its contract is *readable English-like source*.  **Language invariants.** A phase may not change these without explicitly re-opening the language design in its own PROMPT:  | Invariant | Why | |---|---| | `.rb` is the source extension | Tooling, `rb run`, LSP | | `to … end`, `if … end`, `for … end` use `end`, never braces | Core philosophy | | `set x to <expr>` is assignment | Grammar | | `say` prints | 100% of examples depend on it | | `Value` variants are `Nothing/Number/Text/YesNo/List/Record/Object/Function/Builtin` | Public API, `redblue::Value` is exported | | Errors are `Error::{Lexer,Parser,Analyzer,Runtime,Io}` | Public API | | Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax | Parser tests | | Existing tests in `tests/` keep passing | — |  **Backwards compatibility.** `examples/*.rb` and `modules/*.rb` are the language's specification-by-example. Every phase must keep them working. The gate runs them.  ---  ## 3. Testing mandate — this is where most phases fail  > Every test must assert something that can fail.  Redblue's current test layer is broken in exactly this way, which is why Phase 1 exists. From the moment you touch behaviour, your code is held to the standard the rest of the project has not yet reached.  ### 3.1 Every test must have  1. **A real assertion.** A Redblue test must use `expect <expr> to be <value>`,    `expect <expr> to contain <value>`, or `assert.equal`. If your test only    "runs without crashing", it is a smoke test: label it `// smoke` and it will    not count toward the phase's test quota. 2. **At least one edge case.** The happy path alone is not a test. 3. **A named failure message.** `expect x to be 5` must be able to fail with a    useful message. 4. **Determinism.** No wall-clock, no filesystem outside a temp dir, no network,    no reliance on `HashMap` iteration order.  ### 3.2 Mandatory edge-case matrix  For **every** behaviour you implement or change, test at minimum:  - **Empty / zero / "nothing"** — empty list, empty text, `nothing`, `0`, `""` - **Singleton and boundary** — exactly one element; index `0` and index `len-1` - **Out of bounds** — index `-1`, index `len`, index `999` (must be a clean   runtime error, never a panic, never UB) - **Type mismatch** — number where text expected, record where list expected - **Type coercion boundaries** — `0/0`, `1/0`, `-0.0`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`,   `2^53±1` (precision), `-2^31`, integer overflow past `i64` - **Unicode and escapes** — empty text, `"`, `\`, newline in string,   emoji/CJK/RTL, combining marks, very long strings - **Nesting and recursion** — nested lists, nested records, 3+ closure/object   scopes, mutual recursion - **Duplicate and missing keys** — record with repeated keys, missing field access - **Malformed input** — unterminated string, unclosed `end`, stray token,   empty file, BOM, CRLF line endings, non-UTF8 bytes - **Resource and state** — file that does not exist, permission denied, path with   spaces, deeply nested call stack, infinite loop guard  Pick the rows that apply. A phase that changes the VM must justify in `REPORT.md` which rows are N/A and why.  ### 3.3 Test quota (hard floor, not a target)  The gate enforces a floor: **≥ 3 new `#[test]` functions or ≥ 2 new Redblue `test` blocks**, plus **≥ 1 test named `edge_*`**, **≥ 1 test that asserts a failure is produced**, and **zero** newly-skipped or newly-ignored tests.  Both mandatory tests must be expressible in whichever language you are working in. Write them like this:  | Requirement | Rust | Redblue | |---|---|---| | edge case test | `fn edge_empty_list() { … }` | `test "edge_empty list is rejected" do` | | asserts a failure | `assert!(r.is_err())` / `#[should_panic]` | `try … catch error … end` |  A Redblue test name is a **string literal**, so it is `test "edge_foo"`, with a quote after `test `. Likewise Redblue has no exceptions: `try … catch error` is its failure-catching construct, and a test that faults without one is not asserting anything. Both forms are recognised by the gate.  **Not every phase has a failure channel.** A linter reports problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP emit documents. There is no `Result::Err` to assert on, so the "asserts a failure is produced" rule cannot apply to them. Phases 015, 016 and 017 declare `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the gate **skips that one check and says so**. Every other phase keeps it at full strength — a phase that changes what the interpreter *executes* must prove it produces a clean error when it should.  Deliberately, the count floor is low. It exists to catch "no verification at all", not to judge sufficiency — a hard 6 once failed a genuinely good phase with 5 edge tests, which is the gate being smarter than the reviewer. Judging whether the tests COVER the change is the reviewer's job (AGENTS.md section 5: an untested branch is a MAJOR finding, fixed and re-gated). The gate is the floor — cheap, mechanical, never wrong in the pass direction. The reviewer is the ceiling.  ### 3.5 You must implement, not only test  Most rules in this file measure *verification*. None of them measure *ambition*, so the cheapest way to pass the gate would be to write tests for behaviour that already exists and change no implementation at all. That is not a phase.  Every phase in `rbops/phases.json` from 011 onward declares `must_touch` — the areas where that phase's work must land. The gate fails the phase if **no** changed file falls under any of them:  ```json "must_touch": ["src/"]                 // phases 011-013, 015, 016, 018, 019 "must_touch": ["src/", "modules/"]// phase 014, 024 — either is acceptable "must_touch": ["src/", "bootstrap/"]    // phases 021-023 "must_touch": ["tests/"]                // phase 020 — a harness, test-only by design ```  Read your own phase's entry before you start. If your finding genuinely cannot be fixed in the declared area, that is a manifest bug: say so in `FINDINGS.md` with the file:line, and fix it in the area the finding names rather than bending the phase. Phase-024 is the worked example — its finding cites both `src/lexer.rs` and `modules/MathUtils.rb`, so both are declared and either is a valid answer.  Note what this rule is **not**: it is a floor, satisfied by one changed line under `src/`. It says "you implemented something", not "you implemented enough". Judging that the change is correct, complete and proportionate is the reviewer's job, and an `edge_*` test that does not actually pin the new behaviour is a BLOCKER finding.  ### 3.4 The four gates, in order  ``` cargo fmt --check          # formatting        (fails on any diff) cargo clippy -- -D warnings# lints, zero warnings tolerated cargo test                 # full suite, 0 failures ./rbops/verify.sh          # project-specific gates ```  ---  ## 4. REPORT.md contract — required, machine-checked  `phases/phase-NNN/REPORT.md` must exist. `verify.sh` rejects the phase without it.  ```markdown # Phase NNN — <title>  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/vm.rs | +40 −12 | call depth counter in call_builtin |  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | edge_call_stack_overflow | out of bounds / resource | | test_zero_division | type/numeric boundary |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test | 42 passed, 0 failed | | rbops/verify.sh | pass |  ## Invariants touched - None  /  - `expect` now returns a catchable Runtime error (was: process abort)  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - No stack-depth limit for mutually recursive objects yet → FINDINGS.md ```  A REPORT.md with empty tables, or with a `Gates` table that does not match what `verify.sh` printed, is a **fake completion** and will be caught by the auditor.  ---  ## 5. Reviewer contract  The reviewer (`.opencode/agent/reviewer.md`) is **read-only**: no edit, no bash. It reviews the phase diff after the gate is green and emits `FINDINGS:`. Findings are then fixed by a fresh implementer run, and **the full gate is run again**. A phase with open BLOCKER/MAJOR findings is not `.done`.  The reviewer must specifically hunt for:  - **Fake completion** — REPORT.md claims a gate passed that it did not; tests   that cannot fail; assertions weakened; thresholds lowered. - **Panic paths** — any reachable `panic!`/`unwrap`/indexing/`unreachable!`. - **Unbounded resource use** — no recursion limit, no loop guard, no output cap. - **Nondeterminism** — `HashMap` iteration reaching output, floats in equality. - **Error-message regressions** — a previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-084  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-084 — Resolve the reviewer agent's permissions where opencode can actually see them  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `blocker` | | Risk | `medium` | | Depends on | _none_ | | Timeout | 90 min | | Must change | `tests/` |  ## You must change `tests/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [tests/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` is 213 bytes and declares NO `agent` key - `jq -r 'has("agent")'` on it prints false. It sets only `default_agent: "build"` and a permission block whose `edit` and `bash` are both `allow`. The redblue checkout has no opencode.json (`ls redblue/opencode.json` -> No such file or directory). rbops/dispatch.sh:786 invokes opencode as `--dir "$PROJECT_DIR" --agent reviewer`; opencode resolves configuration from the working directory upward and then the global file, so the name `reviewer` is not found there and opencode falls back to `default_agent: build` - a configuration that grants edit and bash. The reviewer is contractually read-only: rbops/agents/reviewer.md opens 'You are READ-ONLY: you cannot edit files or run commands', and the pipeline's own opencode.json sets `agent.reviewer.permission` to `edit: deny, bash: deny`. NEITHER is in the resolution path. dispatch.sh:727-732 already acknowledges the resolution failure in prose and inlines the CONTRACT, which is why every phase has had one - but the PERMISSIONS remain unenforced, so the single component whose job is to catch a compromised phase is the one component that is allowed to edit it, and nothing in the gate would notice if it did. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Ship the agent definitions inside the checkout opencode resolves, so `--agent reviewer` and `--agent verifier` carry their declared permissions instead of inheriting the global build agent's.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-084    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] the project ships an opencode.json whose `agent.reviewer.permission` denies BOTH edit and bash, and whose `agent.verifier.permission` allows both - [ ] a test reads that file and asserts all four permission values, so removing a single deny fails `cargo test` - [ ] a test asserts the file declares no `default_agent`, so the implementer agent cannot be quietly repointed - [ ] the file lives at the repository root and NOT under `.opencode/agent/`, which rbops/verify.sh:127 treats as a forbidden path - [ ] REPORT.md records the opencode configuration-resolution rule this relies on (working directory first, then the global file) with a citation, and says plainly that the test can only assert the file's contents, not opencode's resolution - [ ] edge_* tests cover: the file missing, the `agent` key missing, `reviewer` missing, `verifier` missing, and one permission flipped to allow - five distinct failing cases - [ ] `cargo test` reports 0 failures and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean with no `allow(` added  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## CONTINUATION MODE A previous attempt was interrupted and its partial work is already in the tree. DO NOT restart. Inspect what exists, continue from it, finish the phase.  --- ## PREFLIGHT GAPS — fix these first Preflight checked the inherited work against the gate and found:   -   ?[31mFAIL?[0m report missing section: Tests added   - ?[31m║ PREFLIGHT FAIL   — phase-084           ║?[0m  Add what is missing (tests, failure assertions, report) so the gate passes at the end.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-084/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 084 — Resolve the reviewer agent's permissions where opencode can actually see them  ## State of the tree this run inherited  The phase's production change was already committed. This run started by running the gates on the tree as delivered, before editing anything:  | Gate as delivered | Result | |---|---| | `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass — exit 0 | | `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass — exit 0 | | `cargo test --all-targets` | **red — 1 failure** |  The failure was not in this phase's own file. `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` rejects any commit in `HEAD`'s history whose subject marks unreviewed work and that is not on its acknowledgement list, and it rejects entries naming commits this history does not contain. One entry, one line, was wrong:  ``` unreviewed-work commits selected: 23 | acknowledged: 23 | unacknowledged: 1 | stale entries: 1   | UNACKNOWLEDGED: 48d5bf0183fac60b08e50633aab0aee1b8e846d9  resume phase-084: adopt preserved work   | STALE: 05c77d1a0e2860b1970b4cee93577fefd462aa02 ```  The entry named `05c77d1…`, the resume commit of the *previous* delivery of this phase, which is not in this history — measured this run: `git merge-base --is-ancestor 05c77d1a0e2860b1970b4cee93577fefd462aa02 HEAD` exits 1. The commit this history actually carries is `48d5bf0…`, `git show --numstat 48d5bf0` → `+820/-0`, 5 files, none under `src/`.  That is the fourth delivery of this phase to pay the same one-line repair (FINDINGS.md records the sequence), so this run also added the missing case to the shipped suite. Details in "The one red test" and "The hole the suite had".  ## What changed  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | `tests/agent_permissions_test.rs` | +52 −0 | `check_no_grants_beyond` rejects any permission key on the reviewer beyond `edit`/`bash` unless it too denies, plus `edge_a_tool_granted_to_the_reviewer_besides_edit_and_bash_is_rejected`. This is this run's only production-behaviour change. | | `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` | +1 −1 | The acknowledgement entry repointed from the stale `05c77d1…` to `48d5bf0…`, with figures from `git show --numstat 48d5bf0`, and the same SHA added to `MEASURED`. | | `phases/phase-084/REPORT.md` | rewritten | This file. | | `phases/phase-084/FINDINGS.md` | +21 −12 | Delivery 5 of the acknowledgement treadmill; the `edit`/`bash`-only finding restated against the new rule. |  Delivered in the commit this run inherited, unchanged by it:  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | `opencode.json` | +21 −0 | **New**, at the repository root. `agent.reviewer.permission` = `edit: "deny"`, `bash: "deny"`; `agent.verifier.permission` = `edit: "allow"`, `bash: "allow"`. No `default_agent`, no top-level `permission` block. | | `tests/agent_permissions_test.rs` | +395 −0 | 14 `#[test]` functions; 11 drive the pure checker over deliberately broken documents and assert a failure is produced. |  This run's diff, measured:  ``` $ git diff --numstat -- '*.rs' 52?0?tests/agent_permissions_test.rs 2?2?tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs $ git diff --name-only -- src | wc -l 0 $ git diff -- '*.rs' | grep -c "allow(clippy::" 0 $ git diff -- '*.rs' | grep -cE '#\[ignore|// *skip|\.skip\(' 0 ```  Both changed Rust files are under `tests/`, satisfying this phase's `must_touch: ["tests/"]`. No file under `src/`, `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/` or `rbops/phases.json` is in the diff. No test was deleted, skipped, ignored or weakened; no assertion was loosened and no threshold moved.  ### Why the file is at the root and not under `.opencode/`  `opencode.json` is the project-config filename opencode looks for at the working directory. `.opencode/agent/` holds *agent definition* files (`<name>.md`), not config, and this project's gate treats that directory as a forbidden path. `project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root` asserts the file's parent is the repository root, that no competing copy sits under `.opencode/agent/` or `.opencode/`, and that the file satisfies the permission contract.  ## The configuration-resolution rule this relies on  opencode resolves configuration in two scopes, project first:  > | Scope | Path | > |---|---| > | Project config | `./opencode.json`, `./opencode.jsonc`, or `.opencode/opencode.json`, walked up from the working directory to the worktree root | > | Global config | `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` or `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc` | > > Configs from each scope are deep-merged. Project overrides global.  Citation: opencode's configuration reference, section **"Where files live"**, published alongside the JSON Schema at <https://opencode.ai/config.json>. The machine-checkable half of that citation is the schema, which the shipped file was validated against with `jsonschema` 4.10.3 (FINDINGS.md): **SCHEMA VALID**. It confirms the three claims the fix rests on — `Config.agent`'s additional properties are `AgentConfig`, so arbitrarily named agents are legal; `AgentConfig.mode` enumerates `["subagent","primary","all"]`; and `AgentConfig.permission` accepts `edit` and `bash` with values from the `PermissionActionConfig` enum `["ask","allow","deny"]`. opencode hard-fails to start on a config that fails this schema, so it was checked rather than assumed.  Because the merge is *deep*, the global file's permissive top-level `permission` block still contributes keys underneath this file. Per-agent `permission:` overrides the top-level block, and it is declared here for **both** tools on **both** agents rather than relying on an override for one — which is why the tests assert all four values individually. The file declares no `build` agent and no top-level `permission` block, so the implementer agent still resolves entirely from the global config, exactly as before this phase.  **What these tests cannot prove.** They read a file and assert its contents. They do not run `opencode`, so they cannot prove which file opencode opened, or that a given opencode build resolves the name `reviewer` to this definition. A future opencode that changed its resolution order would leave this suite green while the reviewer again ran with edit access. The tests prove the file says the right thing, and they fail the moment a `deny` is dropped, an agent is renamed, a third tool is granted to the reviewer, or a `default_agent` is reintroduced. Closing the remaining gap needs `rbops/dispatch.sh` to assert the resolved agent's permissions at startup — recorded in FINDINGS.md, not attempted here, since `rbops/` is off-limits.  ## Tests  | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | `project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root` | file present, at root, no forbidden-path or duplicate copy | | `project_config_pins_reviewer_read_only_and_verifier_privileged` | asserts all four permission values individually | | `project_config_declares_no_default_agent` | `default_agent` absent — the implementer agent cannot be repointed | | `edge_missing_config_file_is_rejected` | the reproduction itself: no file → failure | | `edge_missing_agent_key_is_rejected` | empty — `{}` and `{"$schema":"x"}`, both lack `agent` | | `edge_missing_reviewer_is_rejected` | singleton — only verifier declared | | `edge_default_agent_key_is_rejected` | missing keys — a `default_agent` naming `build` or `reviewer` | | `edge_agent_block_of_wrong_type_is_rejected` | type mismatch — `agent` holding an array or a string | | `edge_missing_verifier_is_rejected` | singleton — only reviewer declared | | `edge_permission_flipped_to_allow_is_rejected` | the damaging edit — all four values flipped, each caught by name | | `edge_a_tool_granted_to_the_reviewer_besides_edit_and_bash_is_rejected` | **added this run** — a third tool granted beside the two, which every earlier assertion looked straight past | | `edge_reviewer_without_permission_block_is_rejected` | nesting — `agent.reviewer` declared, `permission` absent | | `edge_permission_value_of_wrong_type_is_rejected` | type mismatch — boolean, number, nested object, null | | `edge_duplicate_permission_key_resolves_to_the_last_value` | duplicate keys — repeated `edit`, last value wins | | `edge_malformed_config_is_rejected` | malformed — empty file, truncated, trailing comma, BOM, array, bare string |  15 `#[test]` functions, 12 of them `edge_*`. **12 assert a failure is produced** (`expect_err` on a `Result`) — counted, not estimated. The five failing documents the phase requires are present as separate tests: file missing, `agent` key missing, `reviewer` missing, `verifier` missing, one permission flipped to allow.  ### The one red test  `unreviewed_work_in_head_history_is_acknowledged`, failing at `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:474` before any edit in this run, for the reason given above. The entry at line 88 now names `48d5bf0…`, the SHA this history contains, with figures taken from `git show --numstat 48d5bf0` and a reason recording the four SHAs it replaces; the same SHA is in `MEASURED`, which that file requires to name every commit it was measured on. No assertion was touched, nothing skipped, no threshold moved. After the change:  ``` selected: 23 | acknowledged: 23 | unacknowledged: 0 | stale entries: 0 test result: ok. 13 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored ```  ### The hole the suite had  Before writing anything, the delivered suite was probed for a way a reviewer could be given a tool without a single assertion noticing. It had one: `check_agent` looks up exactly `TOOLS`, so a third key added beside them is never visited. `{"edit":"deny","bash":"deny","webfetch":"allow"}` satisfied every test in the file. The new test was written first and failed for that reason, not for a missing import:  ``` test edge_a_tool_granted_to_the_reviewer_besides_edit_and_bash_is_rejected ... FAILED panicked at tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:385:57: the reviewer is read-only: `permission.webfetch` must not be grantable: () ```  (The line number is where that assertion stood before the fix was added below; the test itself is at `tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:415`.) `()` is `check_document` returning `Ok(())`. The fix is `check_no_grants_beyond` (`tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:103`), called from `check_document` after the reviewer's two pinned tools: every remaining key in `agent.reviewer.permission` must hold `deny`. It is scoped to the reviewer — the verifier is privileged by contract, so an extra key there is not a defect.  ### Mutation check, run this session on the shipped file, restored after  **Mutation 1 — one `deny` becomes `allow`** (`"bash": "deny"` → `"allow"`):  ``` test edge_permission_flipped_to_allow_is_rejected ... FAILED test project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root ... FAILED test project_config_pins_reviewer_read_only_and_verifier_privileged ... FAILED test result: FAILED. 11 passed; 3 failed; 0 ignored ```  **Mutation 2 — a `default_agent` is inserted** pointing at `reviewer`:  ``` test edge_permission_flipped_to_allow_is_rejected ... FAILED test project_config_declares_no_default_agent ... FAILED test project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root ... FAILED test result: FAILED. 11 passed; 3 failed; 0 ignored ```  In both, the third casualty is `check_file` seeing a document that no longer satisfies the contract — the redundant path by design. The file was restored from `target/tmp/opencode.json.bak` and confirmed byte-for-byte (`cmp` → identical) before the final gate run.  ## Gates  | Gate | Result | |---|---| | `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | **pass** — exit 0, no output, no diff | | `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | **pass** — exit 0, `Finished dev profile`, no warnings; `git diff -- '*.rs' \| grep -c "allow(clippy::"` → `0` | | `cargo test --all-targets` | **pass** — exit 0, **1572 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored** across 60 test binaries (run with `--no-fail-fast`, log at `target/tmp/full_test.log`; `grep -c "FAILED\|^error:"` → `0`) | | `./rbops/verify.sh phase-084` | **NOT RUN — absent from this checkout** |  On the fourth gate: `./rbops/verify.sh` does not exist here. `ls -d rbops .opencode` reports `No such file or directory` for both (measured this run, before any edit), and the pipe